            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the issue!\r\n\r\nI can indeed confirm. The reason this happens is because of [how 'mini.files' tracks which operation need to be done](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/f54410c7f866f51711118fd82274043cf731b63d/doc/mini-files.txt#L234-L245). Here is what is going on:\r\n- Initially explorer contains lines of something like `/1/hello.c` and `/2/hello.h`. Here 1 and 2 are unique path identifiers which are stored for each session.\r\n- First renaming is deduced when synchronizing the following lines: `/1/main.c` and `/2/hello.h`. 'mini.files' sees that the file `1` doesn't match what it has stored and deduces that file should be renamed. And successfully renames. Lines after that are `/2/hello.h` and `/3/main.c` as 'main.c' is a different path on disk.\r\n- *Now*, after undo lines are the same as they were initially. During synchronization 'mini.files' sees that file `3` was present and now is missing thus deciding it needs to be deleted. I *think* it should also suggest creating file `2` in this situation, but it does not.\r\n\r\nI'll see what is the best approach in this situation.",
